# =============================================================================
# JARVIS .gitignore
#
# POLICY CHANGE 2026-08-26. The repo went PRIVATE and the user directed that
# EVERYTHING be tracked — training corpus, personal notes, client work, the
# capture queue, ChromaDB, research papers, machine-local settings. The previous
# policy excluded most of that for privacy or size reasons; those exclusions are
# gone, along with the comments that justified them.
#
# WHAT REMAINS EXCLUDED IS A SHORT LIST, and none of it is excluded for privacy:
#
#   .venv/      2.4 GB and 100% regenerable from requirements.txt. Not a
#               judgment call — GitHub rejects any file over 100 MB, and a venv
#               is platform-specific, so it would be useless on another machine
#               even if it fit.
#   secrets     .env / *.key / *.pem. A private repo is NOT a secret store: it
#               can be forked, shared, granted to someone later, or flipped
#               public by accident — and a leaked key stays in history forever.
#               This repo already learned that the hard way (an OpenRouter key
#               sat in jarvis.ps1 in public history from 2026-04-29). None exist
#               on disk today, verified 2026-08-26; this keeps it that way.
#   build noise __pycache__, *.pyc, caches. Byte-for-byte regenerable, churns
#               every run, and would make every diff unreadable.
#
#   client IP    See the block below. The user reversed course on this within
#                the same session and the reversal is the correct call.
# =============================================================================

# -- CLIENT IP — never committed, private repo or not -------------------------
# The 2026-08-22 never-commit rule STANDS. It was briefly lifted on 2026-08-26
# when the repo went private, then reinstated by the user minutes later, before
# anything was pushed. Recorded rather than silently reverted.
#
# WHY PRIVATE IS NOT ENOUGH: a private repo is still GitHub's servers, not
# Celebal's. It can be forked, shared, granted to someone later, or flipped
# public by accident, and git history is extremely hard to purge afterwards.
# This is an employment/contract question, not a technical one.
#
# SCOPE, decided by the user 2026-08-26 after being shown both locations:
# exclude the raw repo, KEEP the corpus. Their words: "don't push the deepclone
# code, but remove nothing from the corpus data."
#
#   1. client_work/  PROJECT FILES EXCLUDED, PER-PROJECT LEARNINGS TRACKED
#      (revised by the user 2026-09-02; supersedes the blanket exclusion).
#      Still excluded: 16 MB of verbatim BUPA DeepClone source — the framework
#      itself, its git history, its CI config. It carries its own .git, so a
#      plain `git add` would turn it into a broken gitlink, the same failure
#      ai_model_repos/OpenClaude had. The pattern below keeps that ignored.
#      Now tracked: each project's own learnings file/folder, because those are
#      the user's WRITING about the work, not the client's code — and they are
#      no longer distilled into knowledge/Data Engineering/Data_Engineering_
#      Lessons.md, which was making that file grow without bound as projects
#      accumulate. Learnings now live with their project and are cited in place.
#
#      Mechanics note: `client_work/` alone could NOT be selectively re-included,
#      because git cannot re-include a file whose parent DIRECTORY is excluded.
#      Hence `client_work/**` plus explicit directory re-inclusions. Verified in
#      a scratch repo against both layouts (nested folder and flat file).
#
#   2. jarvis_data/training_corpus/  TRACKED, deliberately and with the tradeoff
#      understood. 947 of its blended records (3,127 as of 2026-09-11; the count
#      here read 3,064 and went stale — check with `wc -l`, don't trust this
#      line) — ~2.58 M chars — ARE client
#      text: engineer_corpus.py and personalization_corpus.py lift source files
#      out of client_work/ and chunk them, so these records are the source text
#      itself, not summaries of it. Roughly 60% of the raw client volume travels
#      this way even though the repo above does not.
#
#      This was raised twice and confirmed twice. It is the user's call as the
#      employee. Do not silently re-exclude it, and do not re-argue it — but if
#      a future reader is deciding whether to widen the corpus sources further,
#      this is the constraint they should know exists.
#
# NOTE: `databricks.yml` at the repo root is NOT client material — it is the
# user's own JARVIS learning bundle (bundle name "JARVIS", personal workspace).
# The user guessed it was the client file; checked 2026-08-26, it is not.
client_work/**
!client_work/*/
!client_work/*/session_learnings/
!client_work/*/session_learnings/**
!client_work/*/SESSION_LEARNINGS.md
# =============================================================================

# -- Virtualenv (see header: size + platform-specificity, not privacy) --------
.venv/
venv/
env/

# -- Secrets (see header: never, regardless of repo visibility) ---------------
.env
.env.local
*.pem
*.key

# -- Regenerable build noise --------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
node_modules/
dist/
build/

# -- OS / editor junk ---------------------------------------------------------
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*:Zone.Identifier
*:sec.endpointdlp

# -- Agent host settings (2026-09-08: LEAK REMEDIATION) -----------------------
# .claude/settings.json was TRACKED and pushed (commit b7bfbe6) carrying a live
# github_pat_ inside a permissions allowlist entry — "Bash(GH_TOKEN=\"...\")".
# The token leaked through an APPROVED COMMAND STRING, not a config value, which
# is why no secret-shaped config key ever showed up in an audit.
#
# .agent/hooks.manifest.json already declared this file "machine-local and
# gitignored" — that was the intent and it was simply never true. Now it is.
# bootstrap_jarvis.py rehydrates this file from the manifest, so untracking it
# loses nothing.
.claude/settings.json
.claude/settings.local.json

# -- Derived calibration (a PROJECTION per KB 548) ----------------------------
# Per-model chars-per-token ratios learned from provider usage.prompt_tokens.
# Machine-local and regenerable by simply using the system — syncing it would
# repeat the mistake of git-tracking cognitive_index.sqlite3 and chromadb/,
# which is how the KB/index count drift (544 vs 533) went unnoticed.
jarvis_data/token_ratios.json

# -- Context Ledger (per-session verbatim archive of evicted context) --------
# Append-only spans written before compaction evicts them, keyed by
# content-addressed handle. Machine-local working memory, not the mind: the
# authoritative record is knowledge_base.jsonl. Grows with session length and
# is meaningless on another device (session ids are per-process).
jarvis_data/context/

# -- Local-only PROJECTIONS (2026-09-08) --------------------------------------
# Derived from knowledge_base.jsonl and rebuilt where they are read, so syncing
# them only churns binary diffs and opens staleness windows.
#   cognitive_index.sqlite3      rebuilt by scripts/profile_synth.py
#   behavioral_state_model.jsonl rebuilt by agent/correlation.py each consolidation
# scripts/check_projections.py now detects staleness; brain/projections.py
# surfaces it in the boot inhale.
#
# ⚠️ DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED HERE — do not "finish the job" by adding these:
#   cognitive_profile.md  }  PROJECTION-AS-TRANSPORT. js-workspace-rule.md:21-22
#   activity_digest.md    }  tells the personal laptop to READ these at boot, and
#                            line 25 records why it cannot rebuild them: "there is
#                            NO per-prompt capture here (Antigravity has no hook
#                            system)". No local queue => no digest. Untracking
#                            them BLINDS that machine.
#   chromadb/               CORRECTED 2026-09-10 — the line this replaces was
#                            wrong on the load-bearing fact and I (Claude) wrote
#                            it. It claimed "research_papers is not regenerable
#                            — the source PDFs are gitignored too." Verified:
#                            all 24 source PDFs ARE tracked (git ls-files
#                            research_papers/ | grep -c '.pdf$' => 24; `git
#                            check-ignore` matches nothing under that dir).
#                            BOTH halves of chromadb/ rebuild:
#                              jarvis_memory     -> scripts/index_memory.py
#                              research_papers   -> scripts/ingest.py <pdf>
#                                                   --collection research_papers,
#                                                   once per PDF (no batch mode
#                                                   exists yet — 24 calls today)
#                            chromadb/ stays tracked anyway, per CLAUDE.md's
#                            "don't stage binaries" rule this file itself
#                            states elsewhere: a 22 MB regenerable index isn't
#                            worth committing OR worth ripping out mid-project.
#                            Tenth prose-vs-code drift found this week (KB 548
#                            family) — and the first of the ten where I was
#                            checking someone else's earlier claim and simply
#                            repeated it without re-verifying.
jarvis_data/cognitive_index.sqlite3
jarvis_data/behavioral_state_model.jsonl

# -- Domain labels (2026-09-08) -----------------------------------------------
# The AUTHORITATIVE domain label per captured turn, from the embedding classifier.
# A PROJECTION: rebuilt by `python3 scripts/relabel_domains.py`, idempotent, safe
# to delete. observation_queue.jsonl is the FACT and is never rewritten -- the
# labels live beside it precisely so the log stays append-only.
# Gated by a 28-turn hand-labelled gold set; check_projections.py fails below 80%.
jarvis_data/domain_labels.jsonl

# -- Hearth runtime (2026-09-08) ----------------------------------------------
# The hearth is the one always-on process; these three are per-machine facts
# about THIS host's copy of it, not about the mind:
#   .hearth_token   a shared secret. Committing it would publish the key to the
#                   local API — the third credential leak in this project, and
#                   the first one that would be entirely self-inflicted.
#   .hearth.pid     a process id. Meaningless anywhere else.
#   .hearth_jobs.json  last-run times per scheduled job (a PROJECTION: losing it
#                   costs at most one early run).
#   hearth.log      detached-mode stdout.
jarvis_data/.hearth_token
jarvis_data/.hearth.pid
jarvis_data/.hearth_jobs.json
jarvis_data/hearth.log

# -- Codex ingestion watermark — MACHINE-LOCAL (2026-09-11) -------------------
# {"file": "<rollout basename>", "ts": ...} indexing which of THIS machine's
# ~/.codex/sessions/ rollouts have been ingested. Rollout filenames are local to
# the machine that produced them, so syncing this file carries entries naming
# transcripts the other machine does not have, while saying nothing about its
# own. Harmless but pure noise — and `merge=union` would accumulate it forever.
#
# Contrast .surfaced_watermark.jsonl and .tension_watermark.jsonl, which ARE
# tracked and correctly so: those are keyed by insight id and KB timestamp — the
# same facts on every machine — so sharing them stops an insight already raised
# with the user from being raised again elsewhere.
jarvis_data/.codex_ingest_watermark.jsonl

# -- rfm/ — A SEPARATE REPO, NOT JARVIS CONTENT (2026-09-08) ------------------
# Working clone of https://github.com/Swarajnegi/dadfinanceapp.git — the user's
# personal finance app, built primarily on the personal laptop (Antigravity +
# Codex). It has its own history, its own remote and its own .gitignore.
#
# THIRD INSTANCE OF THE GITLINK FAILURE, after ai_model_repos/OpenClaude and
# client_work/ (see line 46). A nested .git with no ignore rule means a plain
# `git add -A` here stages rfm as a bare commit-SHA pointer with no .gitmodules
# entry: git warns and proceeds, and the pointer is unfetchable — on the
# personal laptop rfm/ then materializes as a permanently empty directory.
# Submodule was considered and declined: that machine clones dadfinanceapp
# directly, so a `git submodule update` ritual on every pull buys nothing.
#
# Also 9.5 MB of built .apk at the repo root, which is its own reason.
rfm/
